<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">People's Republic of Kampuchea</h1><p class="st-meta">1979 CE – 1989 CE · capital Phnom Penh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Vietnamese-backed Cambodian state established after the overthrow of the Khmer Rouge in 1979, isolated internationally for a decade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a> invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/democratic-kampuchea">Democratic Kampuchea</a>, its forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> on 7 January 1979, and the People's Republic of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cambodia">Kampuchea</a> was proclaimed, led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampuchean-united-front-for-national-salvation">Kampuchean United Front for National Salvation</a>, a group of former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> cadres who had defected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heng-samrin">Heng Samrin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hun-sen">Hun Sen</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The PRK was a one-party state under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodian-peoples-party">Kampuchean People's Revolutionary Party</a>, with Vietnamese troops and advisers. It reopened schools, markets, pagodas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> itself, and gathered evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> crimes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuol-sleng">Tuol Sleng</a>. It faced famine in 1979 to 1980 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war from a coalition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> Rouge, royalists and republicans, which held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia's</a> UN seat.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As Soviet aid declined, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a> prepared to withdraw its troops, completed in 1989. That year the state was renamed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-cambodia">State of Cambodia</a>, dropping some socialist features to prepare for a negotiated settlement.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/peoples-republic-of-kampuchea.jpg" alt="People's Republic of Kampuchea" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: See File History, below, for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_People%27s_Republic_of_Kampuchea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1979 CE – 1989 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Phnom Penh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Heng Samrin, Pen Sovan, Hun Sen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khmer</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>PRK</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1984&amp;focus=peoples-republic-of-kampuchea" aria-label="People's Republic of Kampuchea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7566 1746 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where People's Republic of Kampuchea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7886 2002L7886 2002M7886 2002L7846 1656M7886 2002L7886 2002"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7886 2002h0M7846 1656h0M7886 2002h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7886 2002h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7886 2002h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Phnom Penh. <a href="/atlas?year=1984&amp;focus=peoples-republic-of-kampuchea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
